WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.680
I'm originally from South Carolina,

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:09.983
and now I'm all the way over

00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:17.983
in California in Silicon Valley. And here, software is all about optimism. It's not just a means to an end,

00:00:18.543 --> 00:00:19.823
it's this thing that gives us

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:23.887
hope to solve every problem that the world potentially has.

00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:29.807
Moving your sprites around the screen means using a very familiar pattern, the counter pattern.

00:00:29.951 --> 00:00:39.935
This pattern lets you add to the position or rotation of your sprite on each tick of the draw loop, so it looks like it's moving or rotating at different speeds.

00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:44.855
Here's an example we've seen before. Using the counter pattern with the sprite's x

00:00:44.855 --> 00:00:49.695
property, we can control the sprite's velocity, how fast it's moving in a

00:00:49.695 --> 00:00:49.919
particular

00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:57.359
direction. If we add a larger amount to the x position, the sprite looks like it's

00:00:57.359 --> 00:00:59.903
moving faster.

00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:05.903
Adding a smaller amount makes the sprite look like it's moving more slowly.

00:01:05.903 --> 00:01:09.887
You've been using the counter pattern to control a sprite's velocity

00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:15.927
a lot. In programming, when the same pattern happens many times, you can often hide those

00:01:15.927 --> 00:01:19.247
details inside another block.

00:01:19.247 --> 00:01:19.871
In our example,

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:23.111
that block is velocityX.

00:01:23.111 --> 00:01:25.551
The velocityX property hides the details

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:29.011
of the counter pattern that changes the sprite's x position.

00:01:29.011 --> 00:01:29.855
Whatever number you assign

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:34.975
to this property will automatically be added to your sprite's x position on the next tick of the

00:01:34.975 --> 00:01:39.839
draw loop. This program creates a sprite, gives it an

00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:41.839
animation, and then draws it on the screen.

00:01:44.839 --> 00:01:49.823
Now let's give that sprite a velocity by setting its velocityX property to 1,

00:01:49.823 --> 00:01:52.703
just once at the beginning of the program.

00:01:52.703 --> 00:01:54.083
When we rerun the program,

00:01:54.083 --> 00:01:56.723
we see that the sprite's position is updated.

00:01:56.723 --> 00:01:59.043
Under the hood, the velocityX property

00:01:59.043 --> 00:01:59.807
and the counter pattern

00:01:59.807 --> 00:02:05.887
are used to update the sprite's position, moving it across the screen.

00:02:05.887 --> 00:02:09.647
The other new properties that work in a similar way are velocityY,

00:02:09.791 --> 00:02:14.111
which controls velocity in the vertical direction, and rotationSpeed,

00:02:14.111 --> 00:02:16.431
which controls how fast the sprite rotates.

00:02:19.775 --> 00:02:26.495
The under-the-hood behaviors of velocityX, velocityY, and rotationSpeed are all things

00:02:26.495 --> 00:02:28.655
you've tackled in previous lessons.

00:02:28.655 --> 00:02:29.735
Now we get to

00:02:29.759 --> 00:02:34.299
let those blocks handle the details for us and explore new ways of moving sprites on the screen.

00:02:39.743 --> 00:02:41.743
(chime)
